/Title (The Swiss people use approximately one and a half hours per day in travelling and a significant portion of these journeys is in Switzerland; the Swiss also possess a rich culture of using public transport. The likelihood of mobile-play windows is commutes, train rides, waiting times, and brief breaks. The mobility data is giving me the context of gaming, but it is a good fit to the daily life of Swiss people.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 9594 0 R
>>
endobj
9611 0 obj
<<
/Title (Swiss gaming is extensive: 57% of people \(3.6 million people\) play games and 23% play on a daily basis. Smartphone is the most commonly played device and the demographic is divided by age and gender: men generally play more frequently than women though older women are the most active, and young men \(15-19\) are the most active daily. Language regionally, German speaking Switzerland \(24%\), then French speaking Switzerland \(19%\), then Ticino \(15\) have the highest numbers of daily gaming.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 9609 0 R
/Next 9612 0 R
>>
endobj
9612 0 obj
<<
/Title (In the case of multilingual behavior, Switzerland is not a monolingual market two-thirds of the population speaks more than one language every day and English is widely spoken throughout the country, particularly among the younger generation. Federal Statistical Office also indicates that 86 percent believe that the knowledge of more than one national language is a factor in cohesion.)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 9609 0 R
/Prev 9611 0 R
>>
